  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":64,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two classes sit a test. In Class A the marks are tightly clustered around 55; in Class B the marks are spread almost evenly from 20 to 90. Both classes have the same mean mark.\\n\\na Identify the shape of each distribution and compare their spreads. (2)\\n\\nb A parent says Class B is worse because its marks vary more. Write a reply of two or three sentences. (1)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two classes sit a test. In Class A the marks are tightly clustered around 55; in Class B the marks are spread almost evenly from 20 to 90. Both classes have the same mean mark."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Identify the shape of each distribution and compare their spreads. (2)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b A parent says Class B is worse because its marks vary more. Write a reply of two or three sentences. (1)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Class A: approximately symmetric, unimodal, bell-shaped (a roughly normal distribution centred on 55) with a small standard deviation — a tightly packed peak. Class B: approximately uniform (flat/rectangular) between 20 and 90, with a large standard deviation (about 20). Both are centred at the same mean (about 55 in Class A’s description; the common mean sits inside 20–90 for B). (b) ‘Both classes achieved the same average, so on the evidence of averages neither is worse. Class B’s flat spread shows marks are spread evenly — a very mixed intake or uneven preparation, not a weaker class. The right response is to look at which pupils need support and how to provide it, not to criticise the class.’\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct shape of A (symmetric/unimodal/bell) and 1 for B (uniform/flat) with the spread comparison (small σ vs large σ). (b) 1 mark for a reply that separates variability from performance level and proposes an investigation rather than a judgement (any reasonable, context-appropriate reply).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘A is peaked, B is flat’; σ ≈ 0 vs σ ≈ (90 − 20) ÷ √12 ≈ 20; reply mentioning mixed ability/personalised learning.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":64,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Name the shapes; then argue about what σ costs — and doesn’t.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) shape and spread","prompt":"Identify the shape of each distribution and compare their spreads.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Class A bunches at the centre — think bell; Class B is evenly spread — think rectangle.","Same mean, different σ: A small, B large."],"accepted":["Class A is approximately symmetric and unimodal (bell-shaped) with a small spread; Class B is approximately uniform (flat) with a large spread; A: σ ≈ 0, B: nearly uniform over 20–90","A roughly normal/bell, tightly clustered (σ small); B roughly uniform/rectangular (σ large); same mean"],"keywords":[["a","class a"],["symmetric","bell","unimodal","normal","cluster"],["b","class b"],["uniform","flat"],["spread","σ","standard deviation","variab"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b","label":"(b) reply to the parent","prompt":"Write a reply of two or three sentences to the parent.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Variability is not the same as poor performance; the two classes have the same mean.","A wide spread signals a mixed intake or uneven preparation — investigate, don’t condemn."],"accepted":["Variability is not a measure of failure: both classes achieved the same mean, so on average they performed equally. A wide spread suggests Class B has a more mixed intake (or some pupils need different support), not that the class is worse — the school should look at who struggles and why before drawing conclusions.","Both classes average the same marks, so Class B is not worse overall; the flat distribution shows marks spread evenly from 20 to 90, which usually means very mixed prior attainment or uneven preparation — a sign to personalise support, not to judge the class as a whole."],"keywords":[["same mean","average","equal","not worse","no worse"],["mixed","spread","variety","differen","support","individ","tailored","varied"]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Class A: approximately symmetric, unimodal, bell-shaped (a roughly normal distribution centred on 55) with a small standard deviation — a tightly packed peak. Class B: approximately uniform (flat/rectangular) between 20 and 90, with a large standard deviation (about 20). Both are centred at the same mean (about 55 in Class A’s description; the common mean sits inside 20–90 for B). (b) ‘Both classes achieved the same average, so on the evidence of averages neither is worse. Class B’s flat spread shows marks are spread evenly — a very mixed intake or uneven preparation, not a weaker class. The right response is to look at which pupils need support and how to provide it, not to criticise the class.’\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct shape of A (symmetric/unimodal/bell) and 1 for B (uniform/flat) with the spread comparison (small σ vs large σ). (b) 1 mark for a reply that separates variability from performance level and proposes an investigation rather than a judgement (any reasonable, context-appropriate reply).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘A is peaked, B is flat’; σ ≈ 0 vs σ ≈ (90 − 20) ÷ √12 ≈ 20; reply mentioning mixed ability/personalised learning.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Class A: approximately symmetric, unimodal, bell-shaped (a roughly normal distribution centred on 55) with a small standard deviation — a tightly packed peak. Class B: approximately uniform (flat/rectangular) between 20 and 90, with a large standard deviation (about 20). Both are centred at the same mean (about 55 in Class A’s description; the common mean sits inside 20–90 for B). (b) ‘Both classes achieved the same average, so on the evidence of averages neither is worse. Class B’s flat spread shows marks are spread evenly — a very mixed intake or uneven preparation, not a weaker class. The right response is to look at which pupils need support and how to provide it, not to criticise the class.’\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct shape of A (symmetric/unimodal/bell) and 1 for B (uniform/flat) with the spread comparison (small σ vs large σ). (b) 1 mark for a reply that separates variability from performance level and proposes an investigation rather than a judgement (any reasonable, context-appropriate reply).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘A is peaked, B is flat’; σ ≈ 0 vs σ ≈ (90 − 20) ÷ √12 ≈ 20; reply mentioning mixed ability/personalised learning.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Class A: approximately symmetric, unimodal, bell-shaped (a roughly normal distribution centred on 55) with a small standard deviation — a tightly packed peak. Class B: approximately uniform (flat/rectangular) between 20 and 90, with a large standard deviation (about 20). Both are centred at the same mean (about 55 in Class A’s description; the common mean sits inside 20–90 for B). (b) ‘Both classes achieved the same average, so on the evidence of averages neither is worse. Class B’s flat spread shows marks are spread evenly — a very mixed intake or uneven preparation, not a weaker class. The right response is to look at which pupils need support and how to provide it, not to criticise the class.’\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct shape of A (symmetric/unimodal/bell) and 1 for B (uniform/flat) with the spread comparison (small σ vs large σ). (b) 1 mark for a reply that separates variability from performance level and proposes an investigation rather than a judgement (any reasonable, context-appropriate reply).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘A is peaked, B is flat’; σ ≈ 0 vs σ ≈ (90 − 20) ÷ √12 ≈ 20; reply mentioning mixed ability/personalised learning."},{"n":65,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two shops in Lagos sell the same brand of phone. Over one month Shop 1''s daily sales have a five-figure summary of 6, 12, 18, 26, 31 and Shop 2''s have a summary of 2, 9, 17, 24, 40 (both in units sold).\\n\\na Compare the two shops using a measure of centre and a measure of spread, calculating the values you need. (2)\\n\\nb Say which shop you would advise to restock more urgently, and justify your advice. (1)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two shops in Lagos sell the same brand of phone. Over one month Shop 1''s daily sales have a five-figure summary of 6, 12, 18, 26, 31 and Shop 2''s have a summary of 2, 9, 17, 24, 40 (both in units sold)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Compare the two shops using a measure of centre and a measure of spread, calculating the values you need. (2)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Say which shop you would advise to restock more urgently, and justify your advice. (1)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Shop 1: median = 18 units; IQR = 26 − 12 = 14; range = 31 − 6 = 25. Shop 2: median = 17 units; IQR = 24 − 9 = 15; range = 40 − 2 = 38. Centre: the shops are near-identical (18 vs 17 units/day). Spread: Shop 2 is much more variable — its days swing from 2 to 40, with a far larger range, though the IQRs are similar (14 vs 15). (b) Either is defensible: advise restocking Shop 1 more urgently because it is selling more per day (median 18) with steadier demand, so stock turns faster and shelves empty predictably; or Shop 2 because its peak of 40 shows surge days that create stock-out risk and it needs safety stock. The key is a justification tied to the numbers.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both medians (18 and 17) and 1 for a correct spread comparison with values (IQRs 14/15 and/or ranges 25/38). (b) 1 mark: a justified choice either way, anchored in the computed values; an unjustified ‘Shop X’ scores 0.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — spread measured with range only, or IQR only; ‘Shop 2 has more variable sales’; either restock recommendation with matching numbers.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":65,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Compute medians and IQRs/ranges for both, then argue either way.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) centre and spread","prompt":"Compare the two shops using a measure of centre and a measure of spread, calculating the values you need.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["For each shop: median (centre) and IQR or range (spread).","For each shop compute the median, the IQR and the range — then compare centre against spread."],"accepted":["Shop 1: median 18, IQR 14 (range 25); Shop 2: median 17, IQR 15 (range 38) — similar centre, Shop 2 far more variable","medians 18 and 17 (nearly the same); spreads: S1 IQR 14, range 25; S2 IQR 15, range 38 — S2 has the same centre but a much wider spread"],"keywords":[["18"],["17"],["14"],["25","range"],["15"],["38","range"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b","label":"(b) restock advice","prompt":"Say which shop you would advise to restock more urgently, and justify your advice.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Pick one and justify it with the numbers.","Either S1 (higher median, steadier demand) or S2 (risk of 40-unit surge days) is defensible."],"accepted":["Shop 1 — higher median (18 vs 17) and tighter spread (IQR 14, range 25 vs 38), so stock sells faster and more predictably","Shop 2 — its maximum of 40 and wide range mean occasional surge days that can empty shelves; restock more urgently to avoid stock-outs on peak days"],"keywords":[["shop","s1","s2","1","2"]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Shop 1: median = 18 units; IQR = 26 − 12 = 14; range = 31 − 6 = 25. Shop 2: median = 17 units; IQR = 24 − 9 = 15; range = 40 − 2 = 38. Centre: the shops are near-identical (18 vs 17 units/day). Spread: Shop 2 is much more variable — its days swing from 2 to 40, with a far larger range, though the IQRs are similar (14 vs 15). (b) Either is defensible: advise restocking Shop 1 more urgently because it is selling more per day (median 18) with steadier demand, so stock turns faster and shelves empty predictably; or Shop 2 because its peak of 40 shows surge days that create stock-out risk and it needs safety stock. The key is a justification tied to the numbers.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both medians (18 and 17) and 1 for a correct spread comparison with values (IQRs 14/15 and/or ranges 25/38). (b) 1 mark: a justified choice either way, anchored in the computed values; an unjustified ‘Shop X’ scores 0.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — spread measured with range only, or IQR only; ‘Shop 2 has more variable sales’; either restock recommendation with matching numbers.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Shop 1: median = 18 units; IQR = 26 − 12 = 14; range = 31 − 6 = 25. Shop 2: median = 17 units; IQR = 24 − 9 = 15; range = 40 − 2 = 38. Centre: the shops are near-identical (18 vs 17 units/day). Spread: Shop 2 is much more variable — its days swing from 2 to 40, with a far larger range, though the IQRs are similar (14 vs 15). (b) Either is defensible: advise restocking Shop 1 more urgently because it is selling more per day (median 18) with steadier demand, so stock turns faster and shelves empty predictably; or Shop 2 because its peak of 40 shows surge days that create stock-out risk and it needs safety stock. The key is a justification tied to the numbers.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both medians (18 and 17) and 1 for a correct spread comparison with values (IQRs 14/15 and/or ranges 25/38). (b) 1 mark: a justified choice either way, anchored in the computed values; an unjustified ‘Shop X’ scores 0.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — spread measured with range only, or IQR only; ‘Shop 2 has more variable sales’; either restock recommendation with matching numbers.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","statistics"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Shop 1: median = 18 units; IQR = 26 − 12 = 14; range = 31 − 6 = 25. Shop 2: median = 17 units; IQR = 24 − 9 = 15; range = 40 − 2 = 38. Centre: the shops are near-identical (18 vs 17 units/day). Spread: Shop 2 is much more variable — its days swing from 2 to 40, with a far larger range, though the IQRs are similar (14 vs 15). (b) Either is defensible: advise restocking Shop 1 more urgently because it is selling more per day (median 18) with steadier demand, so stock turns faster and shelves empty predictably; or Shop 2 because its peak of 40 shows surge days that create stock-out risk and it needs safety stock. The key is a justification tied to the numbers.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both medians (18 and 17) and 1 for a correct spread comparison with values (IQRs 14/15 and/or ranges 25/38). (b) 1 mark: a justified choice either way, anchored in the computed values; an unjustified ‘Shop X’ scores 0.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — spread measured with range only, or IQR only; ‘Shop 2 has more variable sales’; either restock recommendation with matching numbers."},{"n":66,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The cumulative frequency curve of the marks of 80 students passes through the points (40, 18), (50, 34), (60, 52) and (70, 66).\\n\\na Estimate the median and the interquartile range from the curve. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cumulative frequency curve of the marks of 80 students passes through the points (40, 18), (50, 34), (60, 52) and (70, 66)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Estimate the median and the interquartile range from the curve. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — n = 80. Median: position 40 → on the curve, cf = 40 lies between (50, 34) and (60, 52). Linear interpolation: 50 + (40 − 34) ÷ 18 × 10 = 50 + 3.33 ≈ 53.3 marks. Q1: position 20 → cf = 20 between (40, 18) and (50, 34): 40 + (20 − 18) ÷ 16 × 10 ≈ 41.25. Q3: position 60 → cf = 60 between (60, 52) and (70, 66): 60 + (60 − 52) ÷ 14 × 10 ≈ 65.71. IQR ≈ 65.71 − 41.25 ≈ 24.46 ≈ 24.5 marks.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the median in the range 52–54 (≈53.3 accepted to ±0.5). 1 mark for the IQR ≈ 24.5 (accept 23.5–25.5; Q1 ≈ 41–42 and Q3 ≈ 65–66 both shown). Evidence of interpolation (or an equivalent reading method) required for full credit; rounded readings accepted within the tolerances.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — median ≈ 53, IQR ≈ 24; Q1 ≈ 41, Q3 ≈ 66; any read-off within tolerance without interpolation.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"Cumulative Frequency Curves","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":66,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"n = 80: use cf 20, 40, 60 and interpolate.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) median","prompt":"Estimate the median mark from the curve.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["n = 80, so use the 40th student — read across at cumulative frequency 40 and down to the mark axis.","cf 40 sits between the (50, 34) and (60, 52) points — interpolate inside that segment."],"answer":53.3,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"marks"},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) IQR","prompt":"Estimate the interquartile range (Q3 − Q1) from the curve.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Q1 is read at cumulative frequency 20, between the (40, 18) and (50, 34) points.","Q3 is read at cumulative frequency 60, between the (60, 52) and (70, 66) points — then subtract."],"answer":24.5,"tolerance":0.6,"unit":"marks"}],"marking":"Model answer — n = 80. Median: position 40 → on the curve, cf = 40 lies between (50, 34) and (60, 52). Linear interpolation: 50 + (40 − 34) ÷ 18 × 10 = 50 + 3.33 ≈ 53.3 marks. Q1: position 20 → cf = 20 between (40, 18) and (50, 34): 40 + (20 − 18) ÷ 16 × 10 ≈ 41.25. Q3: position 60 → cf = 60 between (60, 52) and (70, 66): 60 + (60 − 52) ÷ 14 × 10 ≈ 65.71. IQR ≈ 65.71 − 41.25 ≈ 24.46 ≈ 24.5 marks.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the median in the range 52–54 (≈53.3 accepted to ±0.5). 1 mark for the IQR ≈ 24.5 (accept 23.5–25.5; Q1 ≈ 41–42 and Q3 ≈ 65–66 both shown). Evidence of interpolation (or an equivalent reading method) required for full credit; rounded readings accepted within the tolerances.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — median ≈ 53, IQR ≈ 24; Q1 ≈ 41, Q3 ≈ 66; any read-off within tolerance without interpolation.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — n = 80. Median: position 40 → on the curve, cf = 40 lies between (50, 34) and (60, 52). Linear interpolation: 50 + (40 − 34) ÷ 18 × 10 = 50 + 3.33 ≈ 53.3 marks. Q1: position 20 → cf = 20 between (40, 18) and (50, 34): 40 + (20 − 18) ÷ 16 × 10 ≈ 41.25. Q3: position 60 → cf = 60 between (60, 52) and (70, 66): 60 + (60 − 52) ÷ 14 × 10 ≈ 65.71. IQR ≈ 65.71 − 41.25 ≈ 24.46 ≈ 24.5 marks.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the median in the range 52–54 (≈53.3 accepted to ±0.5). 1 mark for the IQR ≈ 24.5 (accept 23.5–25.5; Q1 ≈ 41–42 and Q3 ≈ 65–66 both shown). Evidence of interpolation (or an equivalent reading method) required for full credit; rounded readings accepted within the tolerances.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — median ≈ 53, IQR ≈ 24; Q1 ≈ 41, Q3 ≈ 66; any read-off within tolerance without interpolation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — n = 80. Median: position 40 → on the curve, cf = 40 lies between (50, 34) and (60, 52). Linear interpolation: 50 + (40 − 34) ÷ 18 × 10 = 50 + 3.33 ≈ 53.3 marks. Q1: position 20 → cf = 20 between (40, 18) and (50, 34): 40 + (20 − 18) ÷ 16 × 10 ≈ 41.25. Q3: position 60 → cf = 60 between (60, 52) and (70, 66): 60 + (60 − 52) ÷ 14 × 10 ≈ 65.71. IQR ≈ 65.71 − 41.25 ≈ 24.46 ≈ 24.5 marks.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the median in the range 52–54 (≈53.3 accepted to ±0.5). 1 mark for the IQR ≈ 24.5 (accept 23.5–25.5; Q1 ≈ 41–42 and Q3 ≈ 65–66 both shown). Evidence of interpolation (or an equivalent reading method) required for full credit; rounded readings accepted within the tolerances.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — median ≈ 53, IQR ≈ 24; Q1 ≈ 41, Q3 ≈ 66; any read-off within tolerance without interpolation."},{"n":67,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A stem-and-leaf plot of the number of eggs collected each day on a farm has stems 2, 3, 4 and 5, with 11 leaves in total. The smallest value is 22, the largest is 54, and stem 4 carries six leaves.\\n\\na State the key of the plot, then say whether the data is symmetric or skewed, and how the leaves show it. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stem-and-leaf plot of the number of eggs collected each day on a farm has stems 2, 3, 4 and 5, with 11 leaves in total. The smallest value is 22, the largest is 54, and stem 4 carries six leaves."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a State the key of the plot, then say whether the data is symmetric or skewed, and how the leaves show it. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Key: 2 | 2 means 22 eggs (tens on the stem, units on the leaf) — any stem/leaf pair stated clearly is acceptable. Symmetry: the leaves are NOT symmetric. Six of the eleven days sit on stem 4 (the 40s), and the remaining five stretch from a smallest value of 22 down through the 20s and 30s to 54 — the long tail is on the low side, so the distribution is skewed left (negatively skewed): most days collected eggs in the 40s, with occasional poor days pulling the tail down.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (tens/units identified with ‘eggs’). 1 mark for ‘skewed’ with a reason referencing the leaf layout (bunched 40s, tail towards the 20s/30s → left/negative skew; or, if a candidate reasonably reads the sparse low stems as the tail, accept ‘negatively skewed’). Any answer claiming symmetry must explain the leaves to score the mark — with only stem counts given, skew is the defensible reading.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘skewed left’, ‘negatively skewed’, ‘tail to the left’; key phrased with any stem.","m":2,"df":"easy","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":67,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Six of eleven leaves on one stem — which side is the tail?","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) key","prompt":"State the key of the plot.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["The stem is the tens digit and the leaf the units digit (eggs).","State what one stem-and-leaf unit stands for (eggs)."],"accepted":["2 | 2 means 22 eggs","2|2 means 22 eggs","stem 2 leaf 2 = 22 eggs","2 | 2 = 22 eggs","key: 2 | 2 means 22"],"keywords":[["22"],["means","=","represents","stands"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) symmetric or skewed, and why","prompt":"Say whether the data is symmetric or skewed, and explain how the leaves show it.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Eleven leaves: six are on stem 4; the remaining five are split between stems 2, 3 and 5 (22 to 54).","A bulk on one side with a long tail on the other — which way does the tail point?"],"accepted":["Skewed (to the left / negatively skewed): six of the eleven leaves sit on stem 4 while the remaining five trail off towards stems 2–3 with one leaf on stem 5 — a long tail of low counts pulls one side out.","Negatively skewed — the values bunch in the 40s (six leaves) and spread down towards 22 with only 5 leaves across stems 2, 3 and 5."],"keywords":[["skew"],["left","negative","lower","tail","below"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Key: 2 | 2 means 22 eggs (tens on the stem, units on the leaf) — any stem/leaf pair stated clearly is acceptable. Symmetry: the leaves are NOT symmetric. Six of the eleven days sit on stem 4 (the 40s), and the remaining five stretch from a smallest value of 22 down through the 20s and 30s to 54 — the long tail is on the low side, so the distribution is skewed left (negatively skewed): most days collected eggs in the 40s, with occasional poor days pulling the tail down.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (tens/units identified with ‘eggs’). 1 mark for ‘skewed’ with a reason referencing the leaf layout (bunched 40s, tail towards the 20s/30s → left/negative skew; or, if a candidate reasonably reads the sparse low stems as the tail, accept ‘negatively skewed’). Any answer claiming symmetry must explain the leaves to score the mark — with only stem counts given, skew is the defensible reading.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘skewed left’, ‘negatively skewed’, ‘tail to the left’; key phrased with any stem.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Key: 2 | 2 means 22 eggs (tens on the stem, units on the leaf) — any stem/leaf pair stated clearly is acceptable. Symmetry: the leaves are NOT symmetric. Six of the eleven days sit on stem 4 (the 40s), and the remaining five stretch from a smallest value of 22 down through the 20s and 30s to 54 — the long tail is on the low side, so the distribution is skewed left (negatively skewed): most days collected eggs in the 40s, with occasional poor days pulling the tail down.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (tens/units identified with ‘eggs’). 1 mark for ‘skewed’ with a reason referencing the leaf layout (bunched 40s, tail towards the 20s/30s → left/negative skew; or, if a candidate reasonably reads the sparse low stems as the tail, accept ‘negatively skewed’). Any answer claiming symmetry must explain the leaves to score the mark — with only stem counts given, skew is the defensible reading.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘skewed left’, ‘negatively skewed’, ‘tail to the left’; key phrased with any stem.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Key: 2 | 2 means 22 eggs (tens on the stem, units on the leaf) — any stem/leaf pair stated clearly is acceptable. Symmetry: the leaves are NOT symmetric. Six of the eleven days sit on stem 4 (the 40s), and the remaining five stretch from a smallest value of 22 down through the 20s and 30s to 54 — the long tail is on the low side, so the distribution is skewed left (negatively skewed): most days collected eggs in the 40s, with occasional poor days pulling the tail down.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (tens/units identified with ‘eggs’). 1 mark for ‘skewed’ with a reason referencing the leaf layout (bunched 40s, tail towards the 20s/30s → left/negative skew; or, if a candidate reasonably reads the sparse low stems as the tail, accept ‘negatively skewed’). Any answer claiming symmetry must explain the leaves to score the mark — with only stem counts given, skew is the defensible reading.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘skewed left’, ‘negatively skewed’, ‘tail to the left’; key phrased with any stem."}]'::jsonb) as x
